// The openxr package: raw bindings to OpenXR 1.0 and 1.1, written by hand from
// the specification (registry.khronos.org/OpenXR) and checked against Khronos's
// open headers (openxr.h, openxr_platform.h, OpenXR loader 1.1.65, Apache 2.0),
// the part of it that a program drawing into a headset's swapchain images with
// Vulkan needs: the instance and its extensions, the system (the headset), the
// session, reference spaces, views, swapchains, the frame loop (wait, begin,
// locate views, end), the event queue, paths, and XR_KHR_vulkan_enable2
// (the runtime makes the Vulkan instance and device, and hands over the
// swapchain images as VkImages). It follows the vulkan package's way, and its
// naming:
//
// - A function drops 'xr' and starts lower case: xrCreateInstance is
//   'openxr.createInstance', xrWaitFrame is 'fns.waitFrame'. Its parameters are
//   the specification's, names and order.
// - A type drops 'Xr': XrInstanceCreateInfo is 'openxr.InstanceCreateInfo',
//   its fields named and ordered as the specification has them.
// - A constant drops 'XR_': XR_TYPE_INSTANCE_CREATE_INFO is
//   'openxr.TYPE_INSTANCE_CREATE_INFO', XR_ERROR_RUNTIME_UNAVAILABLE is
//   'openxr.ERROR_RUNTIME_UNAVAILABLE'. Enumerations are i32 (C's int; a
//   runtime may return a value an enumeration would not list), flag sets u64
//   (XrFlags64), an XrResult an i32, an XrTime and an XrDuration an i64, an
//   XrVersion a u64.
// - A function-pointer type drops 'PFN_xr': 'openxr.PFN_CreateInstance'.
//
// Handles are pointers to opaque structs (XR_DEFINE_HANDLE on 64-bit), so
// '0usize as openxr.Session' is XR_NULL_HANDLE; an atom (XrSystemId, XrPath)
// is a u64, 0 being its null.
//
// Every struct is laid out as C lays it out: fields in the specification's
// order, not reordered, not packed. The package's 'layout' test checks each
// size and offset against the C compiler. A struct with a 'type' gives it its
// own value by default and its 'next' defaults to null, so a create-info names
// only what it sets:
//
//   imm ci = openxr.SessionCreateInfo(systemId: systemId, next: &binding as *u8);
//
// Every other pointer field has no default, so each is written, a null one as
// 'null'.
//
// Nothing is linked. The OpenXR loader is opened at run time and every
// function is found through its xrGetInstanceProcAddr, in two steps:
//
//   the module's 'init'            opens the OS's loader (openxr_loader.dll,
//                                  libopenxr_loader.so.1) and finds
//                                  xrGetInstanceProcAddr and the functions
//                                  needing no instance, once, before main:
//                                  'openxr.enumerateInstanceExtensionProperties',
//                                  'openxr.createInstance' and the like. With
//                                  no loader it does not fail:
//                                  'openxr.available()' is false. With a
//                                  loader and no runtime, 'available()' is
//                                  true and the loader answers
//                                  ERROR_RUNTIME_UNAVAILABLE when the
//                                  instance is made
//   InstanceFns.load(instance)     the instance's functions, a table for that
//                                  instance: 'fns.waitFrame(session, ...)'.
//                                  'missing' names how many of the required
//                                  ones were not found; the two extensions'
//                                  functions are optional ('hasVulkanEnable2',
//                                  'hasWin32Time')
//
// Which OpenXR runtime answers is the loader's affair: the XR_RUNTIME_JSON
// environment variable, else the active runtime in the system's configuration.
// The loader itself is found by the OS's search for a library (the program's
// folder, then PATH); no copy of it or of a header is in this repository.
//
// The addresses are called as Cone function references, which use C's calling
// convention. XRAPI_CALL is __stdcall in the header, which on 64-bit Windows
// is the one convention there is; a 32-bit build would need the system
// convention, which Cone does not have yet.
//
// Everything is as C has it: C types, C strings ('*u8', zero-terminated, as a
// string literal is), and structs through pointers. OpenXR passes no struct by
// value, which matters because a call through a function reference passes a
// struct by value as Cone does, not as C does.
//
// A call that fills an array asks twice, as OpenXR's two-call idiom has it:
// once with capacity 0 for the count, once with room for them.
//
// Left out: the action system (action sets, actions, bindings, input state,
// haptics), which is controllers' and the next step; hand and eye tracking,
// passthrough and every vendor extension; the composition layers other than
// the projection layer (quad, cylinder, cube, equirect, depth); the Android
// and D3D bindings; xrInitializeLoaderKHR; XR_KHR_vulkan_enable (the older
// extension; XR_KHR_vulkan_enable2 is the one this package binds); debug
// utils; and the loader's negotiation interface.
//
// Its files are one module:
//
//   handles.cone       versions, handles, XrResult's values and names, session states
//   constants.cone     the constants: structure types, enumerations, flag bits
//   structs.cone       structs: geometry, instance, events, system, session,
//                      views, swapchains, the frame loop, XR_KHR_vulkan_enable2
//   fntypes.cone       the functions, as function-reference types
//   load.cone          the OS's loader, the module's 'init', and the
//                      global-level functions (GlobalFns)
//   instancefns.cone   the instance-level functions (InstanceFns)
//
// The layout test's tests/layout.py reads them all, and lists them.

mod openxr;

import libc;
import vulkan;
